faq:
  - q: How Much Does Engine Modification Cost vs. Leaving It Stock?
    body: >-
      Engine modification cost varies by category, part selection, and labor scope — a single intake or exhaust upgrade costs less than a multi-component forced induction build. Duft Auto provides a written estimate listing parts and labor before any component gets ordered, so the cost of a build is clear before a decision gets made. Leaving a vehicle stock avoids modification cost entirely but also skips the airflow, exhaust, or tuning changes a modification project targets. Auggie walks through the modification scope and cost directly with the customer before work begins, not after the invoice arrives.
  - q: How Do I Know What Modification My Vehicle Needs?
    items:
      - Interest in intake or exhaust airflow changes
      - Forced induction addition — turbocharger or supercharger
      - ECU tuning after hardware installation
      - Lift kit for ground clearance or towing use
      - Uncertainty about aftermarket part fitment for a specific engine
  - q: What Does the Engine Modification Process Involve at Duft Auto?
    body: >-
      A build starts with a scheduled appointment — no walk-in wait, no guessing when a bay opens up. Auggie confirms part number and fitment against the specific engine, model year, and drivetrain before ordering any modification hardware. A diagnostic baseline gets documented before installation, hardware goes in to manufacturer or component-specific torque spec, and tuning follows once physical components are installed and verified. Findings and next steps get explained in plain language throughout, with a written estimate covering parts and labor before work starts.
